    {
      "className": "help-interface-guide",
      "detail": "How XF Studio's panels, controls and words look and behave.",
      "label": "Interface guide",
      "link": "project-style-guide",
    },
  ],
  "markup": [
    "<p>A <strong>preset</strong> is one complete look; each preset becomes one choice in the game's eye makeup selector. A preset is built from <strong>layers</strong>, which stack like real makeup: the top of the Layers list sits in front.</p><ul><li>Add, duplicate, hide and remove layers from the Layers panel.</li><li>Drag a row's grip, or use <kbd>Alt+↑ / Alt+↓</kbd>, to change the order.</li><li><kbd>F2</kbd> renames the focused row.</li></ul>",
    "<p>Shape the selected layer on the <strong>UV map</strong> or directly on the head.</p><ul><li>Drag a point to reshape the outline; drag inside the shape to move it.</li><li>Double-click the outline in the UV map to add a point.</li><li>Hold <strong>Shift</strong> and drag to rotate the shape, or Shift-wheel to scale it.</li><li>Esc cancels a drag while you're still holding it.</li></ul><p>The Shape, Pigment &amp; edge and Warp panels hold the finer controls.</p>",
    "<p>The head shows your makeup on V. Drag off the makeup to turn the view, use the wheel to zoom and right-drag to pan; <kbd>F</kbd> returns to the front view.</p><p>The 3D view is built from your own Cyberpunk 2077 files the first time. If it isn't ready, the head pane shows the one next step. The UV map, library and Check all work without it.</p>",
    "<p>Each layer has a colour, an opacity and a finish. <strong>Matte</strong>, <strong>Satin</strong> and <strong>Metallic</strong> can go into your mod. <strong>Glossy</strong> and <strong>Colour-shifting</strong> can be built as experiments. <strong>Shimmer</strong> and <strong>Glitter</strong> are being reworked and wait for a check in the game, so the picker shows them only with <strong>Show research tools</strong> on (Settings › Appearance). A layer that already uses one keeps it.</p>",
    "<p>Every change can be undone. <kbd>Ctrl+Z</kbd> undoes, and <kbd>Ctrl+Shift+Z / Ctrl+Y</kbd> redoes. Undo follows what you last edited: a preset or a V. View and lighting changes have their own Undo. The <strong>History</strong> panel names what you last edited and lists your recent changes to it; click any step to go back to it.</p>",
    "<p>Your work saves itself to your local library as you go. <strong>Save version</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl+S</kbd>) keeps a version of your collection there, beside its earlier versions, that you can go back to.</p><p>Opening or importing another collection never replaces the one you had open: it stays in your library, and <strong>Reopen previous</strong> shows it again. Collections never saved as a version are listed under <strong>Recent drafts</strong> in the Library panel, where <strong>Reopen</strong> shows one.</p><p>To keep a separate copy or share looks, export the collection as a file from the Library panel, and import it again on any computer.</p>",
    "<p>Open <strong>Mod package</strong> when your looks are ready.</p><ul><li><strong>Check</strong> lists which presets and layers can become mod files, and names anything that would be left out and why. It needs no game files.</li><li><strong>Build</strong> makes your own <strong>XF Eye Artistry</strong> mod files. Nothing goes into your game until you add it: see <strong>Installing your mod</strong>.</li></ul>",
    "<p>After <strong>Build</strong>, each mod in the result has two buttons.</p><ul><li><strong>Add to my mod manager</strong> shows exactly what would be added and where before anything changes: with Mod Organizer 2, the mod's own folder and one new row in your profile's mod list, at the bottom of the lowest section that doesn't hold frameworks (your separators and every other mod stay where they are). Without Mod Organizer 2, its files go into the game folder: archive\\pc\\mod, and r6\\tweaks for a mod that has TweakXL files. Choose <strong>Add</strong> to go ahead.</li><li><strong>Show in folder</strong> opens the built mod, to install it by hand: copy its <strong>archive</strong> folder (and its <strong>r6</strong> folder, when it has one) into your Cyberpunk 2077 folder, or add it to Vortex as a mod.</li></ul><p>XF Studio never replaces files it didn't put there, never changes your frameworks or other mods, and waits while Mod Organizer 2 is open (it rewrites its mod list when it closes). Adding a newer build replaces only the files it added before. If the eye makeup row is missing, check that <strong>XF Eye Artistry</strong> is switched on in your mod manager. Launch the game through Mod Organizer 2 if you use it. Open <strong>Settings › Game › Mod frameworks</strong>: it shows which <strong>ArchiveXL</strong>, <strong>RED4ext</strong> and <strong>redscript</strong> the game will load, says if one is too old or missing, and links to its latest release on Nexus Mods and GitHub. XF Studio never installs or updates frameworks for you. If something still looks wrong, <strong>Report a problem…</strong> in Help prepares a report for you to review.</p><p>Your game folder and mod manager are chosen in <strong>Settings › Game</strong> (the Settings button at the top right), and <strong>Settings › Game › Mod frameworks</strong> shows whether the frameworks your mod needs are in place.</p>",
    "<p>Everything XF Studio needs to know about your computer is in <strong>Settings</strong>: the Settings button at the top right, or type “Settings” in the command palette (<kbd>Ctrl+K / Ctrl+P / Ctrl+Shift+P</kbd>).</p><ul><li><strong>Game</strong>: your Cyberpunk 2077 folder and how you install mods (Mod Organizer 2 and its profile, or Vortex and the game folder). XF Studio finds these for you, and under <strong>Mod frameworks</strong> shows whether the frameworks your mod needs (ArchiveXL, RED4ext and redscript) are in place.</li><li><strong>Saves</strong>: where your saves are. XF Studio uses the game's own folder, Saved Games\\CD Projekt Red\\Cyberpunk 2077; choose another if you keep them somewhere else.</li><li><strong>Tools</strong>: WolvenKit, which XF Studio sets up for you.</li><li><strong>Appearance</strong>, and <strong>Privacy &amp; diagnostics</strong>.</li></ul><p>Each choice is saved on this computer as you make it.</p>",
    "<p>Load a save from the <strong>Character</strong> panel and the 3D view shows that V's own skin, makeup and face details, eyes, brows, lashes, hair, piercings and body, read from your game and mods. Your looks and library are never changed by loading a save.</p><p>You can change any creator option there too, to check your looks on another face, and save a set of options as a preset to use again. None of this changes the save itself.</p>",
    "<p><strong>Camera &amp; light</strong> frames the 3D view and lights your V.</p><ul><li><strong>Camera</strong>: the front view (<kbd>F</kbd>), the whole body, the character creator's own face and hair cameras, and the field of view.</li><li><strong>Light</strong>: choose a lighting setup. <strong>Character creator</strong> uses the game's own creator lights, to compare with what you see in the game. Make a new setup to change its lights, their colour and their direction on the dial.</li></ul><p>Camera and light are saved with your workspace. They never change your looks or your mod.</p>",
    "<p>When something goes wrong, the notice shows a reference such as <strong>XF-7K3Q</strong> and a <strong>Report this problem</strong> button. You can also report from here, or from the command palette.</p><ul><li>The report is prepared for you to review first. Nothing leaves your computer unless you send it.</li><li>Include each part or turn it off. Personal folder names and e-mail addresses are already replaced.</li><li><strong>Save report</strong> makes one file to attach to a GitHub issue; <strong>Open a GitHub issue</strong> starts one for you.</li><li>XF Studio keeps the last half hour of what it worked out (which mod supplied what), so you don't have to make the problem happen again. <strong>Diagnostic mode</strong> keeps more, for a day.</li></ul><p>Your mods are identified by name, version and download source, never copied. Reports on GitHub are <strong>public</strong>: only include a mod's own files if you made it, or its permissions allow sharing it.</p>",
    "<p>This is a beta: what it doesn't do yet, and what hasn't been tried in the game.</p><p><strong>In your mod</strong></p><ul><li><strong>A masculine V: not tried in the game yet.</strong> Build makes XF Eye Artistry for both a feminine and a masculine V, but the masculine character creator's XF row hasn't been seen in the game yet.</li><li><strong>Shimmer and Glitter are waiting for a check in the game.</strong> Both were reworked after they didn't look right in the game (Shimmer showed as glossy vinyl), and the new versions haven't been tried in the game yet. Until they pass, the finish picker offers them only with <strong>Show research tools</strong> on; a layer that already uses one keeps it, and Glitter isn't built into your mod.</li><li><strong>Some finishes are still experiments.</strong> Glossy doesn't look different from Satin yet. A Colour-shifting look holds only one Colour-shifting layer. <strong>Colours and finishes</strong> says which finishes go into your mod.</li><li><strong>Full-face masks: not tried in the game yet.</strong> An item that hides V's whole head should hide the makeup with it. An ordinary helmet covers the makeup, as it covers the game's own.</li><li><strong>Expression sets are checked by Build but not tried in photo mode yet.</strong></li></ul><p>If something doesn't work in the game, use <strong>Report a problem…</strong> at the bottom of Help.</p><p><strong>In the 3D view</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Finishes look closer to each other in the game.</strong> Under the character creator's soft light they barely separate; the 3D view shows them further apart.</li><li><strong>Face movement: not checked against the game yet.</strong> The idle's face, the blink and the live face in Expressions come from your game's own facial setup, but may not match the game exactly.</li><li><strong>Clothes from mods aren't drawn.</strong> The 3D view dresses V in the game's own clothes from your save; clothes that mods add aren't shown yet.</li></ul><p><strong>Installing and updating</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Updates are downloaded by hand.</strong> XF Studio tells you when a newer version is out (it looks when it starts; <strong>Settings › Updates</strong> turns that off), and <strong>Check for updates</strong> in Help looks now. New versions are on the <strong>Releases</strong> page on GitHub. Export your looks before installing one.</li><li><strong>Windows may warn before the setup runs,</strong> because the app isn't signed yet. Choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</li></ul>",
    "<p>Drag a panel's tab to dock it beside another, or onto a floating spot. The <strong>Panels</strong> button in the header opens or closes panels in the current tab. To reset a tab, open its menu (right-click the layout tab or press Shift+F10 while it's focused) and choose <strong>Reset to default</strong>. <kbd>F6</kbd> moves the keyboard focus between regions.</p>",
  ],
